<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The parties married in December 2018. The marriage ran into difficulty within months, with the wife alleging cruelty and harassment; the parties separated in April 2019, and criminal proceedings followed on allegations under provisions including Sections 498A and 406 of the IPC, alongside proceedings under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005. In May 2019 the wife sought interim maintenance.</p>
<p>The Family Court assessed the husband&rsquo;s monthly income in the range of &#8377;80,000&ndash;90,000 and fixed interim maintenance at &#8377;15,000 per month. Dissatisfied, the wife carried the matter to the Delhi High Court in revision &mdash; CRL.REV.P.(MAT.) 598/2026 &mdash; seeking enhancement. Her case rested substantially on the respondent&rsquo;s bank transactions, which she said revealed far greater financial capacity, and on her own expenses, including the cost of preparing for the judicial services examinations.</p>

<h2>What the High Court held</h2>
<p>Justice Madhu Jain dismissed the revision on 29 September 2026. Two strands of reasoning stand out.</p>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>No fixed formula.</strong> The determination of maintenance is a holistic exercise. The Family Court weighs the income and assets of both sides, their liabilities, the standard of living during the marriage and the realistic needs of the claimant. No arithmetic shortcut &mdash; a fixed percentage or a mechanical multiplier &mdash; substitutes for that assessment.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Credits are not income.</strong> Transactions in a bank account do not, without more, establish actual disposable income. The nature of each entry matters: receipts may represent turnover rather than profit, borrowings rather than earnings, or circular transfers rather than fresh money. A claim for enhancement built on gross credits, without examining what the entries are, does not demonstrate perversity in the order under challenge.</div>
</div>
<p>Finding neither patent illegality nor perversity in the Family Court&rsquo;s assessment, the High Court declined to interfere &mdash; while preserving the petitioner&rsquo;s liberty to seek modification before the Family Court if circumstances materially change.</p>

<h2>Why the decision matters in daily practice</h2>
<p>Interim maintenance is decided at a stage when evidence is incomplete, and bank statements are often the only hard documents on record. This decision is a reminder that they must be read, not merely counted. For claimants, the lesson is to go beyond totals: identify salary credits, recurring business receipts, rental inflows or investment income, and connect them to a coherent picture of earning capacity. For respondents, the decision underlines the value of explaining entries &mdash; through ledgers, loan documents or tax returns &mdash; rather than leaving the court to draw adverse inferences.</p>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Affidavits:</strong> both sides file detailed affidavits of assets, income and expenditure.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Documents:</strong> bank statements, tax returns and salary records are disclosed and tested against the affidavits.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Assessment:</strong> the Family Court fixes a provisional figure on a holistic view of the material.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Review:</strong> revisional interference is confined to patent illegality or perversity; modification lies on changed circumstances.</div>
</div>

<h2>The revisional threshold</h2>
<p>The judgment also illustrates the narrow channel through which interim maintenance orders are reviewed. The High Court in revision does not sit as a second Family Court. Unless the order ignores material evidence, rests on no evidence, or applies a legally impermissible standard, the figure fixed below will generally stand &mdash; with the parties relegated to the fuller remedy of trial, where maintenance is finally determined on complete evidence. Litigants weighing a challenge to an interim order should therefore assess not whether a higher figure was possible, but whether the order is legally unsustainable.</p>
<div class="note"><p>Practice pointer: a plea for enhancement is strongest when anchored in specific, explained entries &mdash; identified salary or business credits, admitted assets, or demonstrated concealment &mdash; rather than aggregate account turnover. Equally, a respondent who fully discloses and explains his finances makes revisional interference far less likely.</p></div>
